---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const corpfinCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · The financing decision', note: 'Fund projects with debt (cheap, tax-deductible interest, but obligations) or equity (no obligations, but dilution and costlier). The mix is capital structure.' },
    { math: '2 · WACC: the hurdle rate', note: 'Weighted Average Cost of Capital = blend of debt and equity costs, weighted by their shares. The minimum return a project must clear — the discount rate from the NPV lesson, finally sourced.' },
    { math: '3 · Modigliani–Miller: the irrelevance baseline', note: 'In a perfect world (no taxes, no bankruptcy costs), capital structure is IRRELEVANT — the pie is the same size however sliced (Nobel 1985/1990). Everything real comes from the frictions they assumed away.' },
    { math: '4 · Real frictions drive real choices', note: 'Tax shield favours debt; bankruptcy costs and financial distress favour equity; agency costs (managers hoarding cash vs over-borrowing) pull both ways. Observed structures are the equilibrium of these forces.' },
  ],
};

const corpfinQuiz = {
  questions: [
    {
      q: "A firm has market equity of 800, debt of 200, cost of equity 12%, pre-tax cost of debt 6%, and a 25% tax rate. What is its WACC?",
      choices: ["10.8%", "10.5%", "9.0%", "11.2%"],
      answer: 1,
      why: "WACC = 0.8 × 12% + 0.2 × 6% × (1 − 0.25) = 9.6% + 0.9% = 10.5%. Choosing 10.8% means you forgot the tax shield on interest — the single most common exam error.",
    },
    {
      q: "In the frictionless Modigliani–Miller world, what happens to a firm's total value when it swaps equity for debt?",
      choices: [
        "It rises, because debt is cheaper than equity",
        "It falls, because debt adds risk",
        "Nothing changes — investors can replicate leverage themselves",
        "It doubles, because leverage doubles returns",
      ],
      answer: 2,
      why: "Proposition I: with perfect markets the pie is sliced differently but stays the same size. Cheap debt is exactly offset by the rise in the required return on the remaining equity.",
    },
    {
      q: "A supplier offers terms of 2/10 net 30. You can borrow at 10% a year. What should you do?",
      choices: [
        "Pay at day 30 — free credit is free",
        "Pay at day 10 — forgoing the discount costs about 37% annualised",
        "Pay at day 15 as a compromise",
        "It makes no difference to value",
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: "Skipping the discount costs 2 to borrow 98 for 20 extra days: (2/98) × (365/20) ≈ 37% a year. Borrow at 10% and take the discount — you pocket roughly 27 points.",
    },
  ],
};

const cfThreeBuckets = {
  "prompt": "Every corporate-finance decision lands in one of three drawers — file them:",
  "buckets": [
    "which projects to invest in",
    "how to fund them",
    "what to return to owners"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "take the factory with NPV +16.2 or reject it",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "issue bonds or shares for the expansion",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "declare a dividend or repurchase shares",
      "bucket": 2
    },
    {
      "label": "rank projects by profitability index under rationing",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "choose 7% rather than 10% as the hurdle",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "initiate a dividend to signal durable cash",
      "bucket": 2
    }
  ],
  "why": "The third drawer looks trivial and turns out to be a signalling game; the second carries the most famous 'it doesn't matter' result in all of social science."
};

const cfNpvPlotter = {
  "curves": [
    {
      "expr": "300*((1/(1+x)) + (1/(1+x)^2) + (1/(1+x)^3) + (1/(1+x)^4)) - 1000",
      "color": "#1d4ed8",
      "label": "NPV of the project at hurdle rate x",
      "width": 2
    }
  ],
  "x": {
    "min": 0,
    "max": 0.15,
    "label": "hurdle rate"
  },
  "y": {
    "min": -120,
    "max": 260,
    "label": "NPV"
  },
  "height": 300,
  "crossings": true
};

const cfRationBranch = {
  "root": "The budget is 1,000. A needs 300 to return 450 (NPV 150, PI 1.5); B needs 500 to return 700 (NPV 200, PI 1.4); C needs 400 to return 560 (NPV 160, PI 1.4).",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "text": "PI-ranking greedily takes A (300 spent), then B (800 spent). C costs 400 — over budget. What now?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Settle for A + B",
          "next": "combo",
          "feedback": "Total NPV 350 — respectable, but one combination beats it."
        },
        {
          "label": "Swap B out for C: run A + C",
          "next": "combo",
          "feedback": "310 — the swap costs more than it gains."
        },
        {
          "label": "Re-search the combinations from scratch",
          "next": "combo",
          "feedback": "With lumpy, indivisible projects, PI is a heuristic, not an optimizer — combinations must be searched."
        }
      ]
    },
    "combo": {
      "text": "The search finds B + C: 900 spent, NPV 360 — beating A + B's 350 with 100 of budget left idle by choice, not by the ranking."
    }
  },
  "why": "PI ranks efficiently but a lower-ranked pair can be worth more together — which is why the NPV rule, not payback, remains the referee."
};

const cfIrrSpot = {
  "prompt": "One line of this project-briefing is wrong — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "When projects compete, take the highest NPV, not the highest IRR.",
    "A small kiosk can show a spectacular IRR and still add less value than a duller, larger factory.",
    "IRR assumes each year's cash flows are reinvested at the honest, attainable discount rate.",
    "When cash flows change sign more than once, IRR can produce two or more answers.",
    "That is why mining projects — build, harvest, clean up — are the textbook IRR trap."
  ],
  "errorLine": 2,
  "correction": "IRR assumes reinvestment at the IRR itself — a spectacular IRR presumes spectacular reinvestment; only NPV assumes the honest, attainable discount rate.",
  "why": "NPV's reinvestment assumption is the attainable one, and a single sign change too many can mint multiple IRRs with no way to rank them."
};

const cfCostMatch = {
  "prompt": "Pricing the hurdle — pair each input with what it costs or claims:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "Debt",
      "right": "contractually cheap — priority in bankruptcy, and interest is tax-deductible"
    },
    {
      "left": "Equity",
      "right": "the residual claim, paid last, demanding the most — priced with CAPM"
    },
    {
      "left": "The tax shield",
      "right": "(1 − t) applied to interest only; dividends are not deductible"
    },
    {
      "left": "Market values",
      "right": "weights from today's prices — a firm whose shares tripled still re-weights"
    },
    {
      "left": "WACC",
      "right": "the blend that turns the funding mix into the hurdle rate"
    }
  ],
  "why": "The two classic slips — book-value weights and a tax shield on the wrong flow — are exactly what the worked example warns against."
};

const cfPropIIEstimate = {
  "prompt": "Proposition II: with r_A = 10% and r_D = 6%, what return must equity holders demand at D/E = 2?",
  "unit": "% required on equity",
  "answer": 18,
  "factor": 1.15,
  "why": "r_E = r_A + (D/E) × (r_A − r_D) = 10% + 2 × 4% = 18% — and the blend (1/3 × 18% + 2/3 × 6%) still averages 10%. Debt is cheaper per unit, but it makes every remaining unit of equity riskier and more expensive.",
  "context": "Check the blend: one-third at 18% plus two-thirds at 6% still averages exactly 10%."
};

const cfCccCloze = {
  "text": "The worked firm holds inventory %%0%% days (DIO), collects %%1%% days after sale (DSO) and pays suppliers after %%2%% days (DPO): cash is locked up for %%3%% days — on revenue of 7.3 million, roughly 1.3 million of permanent funding.",
  "answers": [
    [
      "60",
      "sixty"
    ],
    [
      "45",
      "forty-five"
    ],
    [
      "40",
      "forty"
    ],
    [
      "65",
      "sixty-five"
    ]
  ],
  "hint": "DIO + DSO − DPO. Squeeze DSO to 30 and you release about 0.3 million — the CFO's cheapest financing."
};

const cfPayoutCompare = {
  "prompt": "The same 10 million returned, two instruments — drag the divider:",
  "before": {
    "label": "Dividend",
    "text": "The share drops to 90 on the ex-date and the holder keeps 90 plus 10 of cash — and a commitment: cutting one is read as distress, while initiating one says 'this cash stream is durable'."
  },
  "after": {
    "label": "Buyback",
    "text": "100,000 shares retired at 100: 900,000 shares now own a 90 million firm — still worth 100 each. Flexible, often taxed as capital gains, and a signal that shares are cheap — or that positive-NPV projects have run out."
  },
  "why": "At fair prices the arithmetic is identical; the differences are institutional — commitment versus flexibility, income versus gains — which is why payout choices signal."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/finance/advanced/corporate-finance">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced Finance lesson. It assumes the foundational lesson <em>Compound Interest
    and the Time Value of Money</em> and the intermediate lessons on Time Value of Money (NPV),
    Financial Statements, Stocks &amp; Bonds, and Diversification &amp; Portfolios — the
    discounting machinery, the balance-sheet literacy and the risk–return grammar this page now
    applies to a live firm.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>appraise projects with NPV, profitability index and IRR — and say exactly where IRR misleads;</li>
    <li>compute a WACC from market-value weights, applying the tax shield to debt and nothing else;</li>
    <li>state Modigliani–Miller's irrelevance result and the homemade-leverage arbitrage behind it;</li>
    <li>measure leverage's amplification with the degree of financial leverage, in both directions;</li>
    <li>read a cash conversion cycle and price trade credit terms before signing them.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    You can now discount a cash flow, price a bond, and explain why diversification kills risk without
    touching expected return. Corporate finance is where those tools meet a live balance sheet and a real
    management team. It answers <strong>three questions: which projects to invest in, how to fund them, and
    what to return to owners</strong>. The first question is applied NPV — you have the machinery already.
    The second is capital structure, and it comes with the most famous "it doesn't matter" result in all of
    social science. The third looks trivial and turns out to be a signalling game. The syllabus unit
    (governance, investing and financing decisions, leverage, working capital) is exactly this territory, and
    we will take each piece in turn.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="file the decisions: invest, fund, or return" widget="sort-buckets" config={cfThreeBuckets} />

  <Widget no="1" caption="capital structure, WACC, and the irrelevance theorem that organises everything" widget="equationStep" config={corpfinCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the cards run the lesson's spine. Card 1 poses the financing
    decision as debt versus equity; card 2 names the blend — WACC — that turns the mix into a
    hurdle rate; card 3 delivers the surprise, Modigliani–Miller's proof that in a perfect world
    the mix does not matter at all; card 4 lists the real frictions that break the perfect world.
    Read card 3 slowly: every real-world argument below is measured against that
    irrelevance baseline.
  </p>

  <h2>Investing decisions: which projects earn their keep</h2>
  <p>
    The rule you know — take projects with positive NPV — hides the real difficulty: choosing the discount
    rate. Watch how sensitive the answer is. A project costs 1,000 today and pays 300 a year for four years.
    At a 10% hurdle, the discount factors are 0.9091, 0.8264, 0.7513 and 0.6830, summing to 3.1699, so the
    present value is 951.0 and the NPV is <strong>−49.0</strong>. Drop the hurdle to 8% and the factors sum to
    3.3121, giving 993.6 — still negative. At 7% the sum is 3.3872, the PV is 1,016.2, and the NPV turns
    <strong>positive at +16.2</strong>. The internal rate of return sits near 7.7%. Whether this project is
    good or bad is not a fact about the project alone; it is a fact about the project <em>relative to its cost
    of capital</em>, which is why the next two sections matter more than the arithmetic.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="steer the hurdle — watch the NPV profile flip" widget="plotter" config={cfNpvPlotter} />
  <p>
    Three refinements cover almost all practical cases. <strong>Mutually exclusive projects</strong>: when you
    can do only one, pick the highest NPV, not the highest IRR — a small kiosk can have a spectacular IRR and
    still add less value than a duller, larger factory. <strong>Capital rationing</strong>: when the budget is
    fixed, rank by the profitability index, PI = PV of inflows ÷ investment. Suppose 1,000 is available and
    project A needs 300 to return 450 (PI 1.5, NPV 150), B needs 500 to return 700 (PI 1.4, NPV 200), and C
    needs 400 to return 560 (PI 1.4, NPV 160). Greedy PI-ranking takes A then B (350) and leaves no room for C —
    but B plus C together yield 360 on 900 spent. PI is a superb heuristic, not an optimizer; with "lumpy"
    indivisible projects you sometimes have to search combinations. <strong>Timing</strong>: the NPV rule
    already handles it, which is precisely why we use NPV and not payback period.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="spend the 1,000 — find the best combination" widget="branch" config={cfRationBranch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> IRR is not "the return you earn on the project." Implicitly it assumes
  intermediate cash flows are reinvested <em>at the IRR itself</em> — at 40% IRR that assumption is fantasy.
  NPV assumes reinvestment at the discount rate, which is the honest, attainable rate. IRR also breaks down
  entirely when cash flows change sign more than once (mining projects: build, harvest, clean up), producing
  two or more IRRs and no way to choose between them.</p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="find the faulty line — the IRR briefing" widget="spot-error" config={cfIrrSpot} />

  <h2>The cost of capital: pricing debt and equity</h2>
  <p>
    The hurdle rate comes from the funding mix. <strong>Debt</strong> is contractually cheap: lenders take
    priority in bankruptcy, so they demand less — and interest is tax-deductible, so a 6% coupon costs an
    after-tax 4.5% at a 25% tax rate. <strong>Equity</strong> is the residual claim: shareholders are paid
    last, so they demand the most, estimated with the CAPM (risk-free rate plus beta times the equity risk
    premium). The blend is the <strong>Weighted Average Cost of Capital</strong>:
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="pair each input with its price" widget="match" config={cfCostMatch} />
  <p>
    WACC = (E/V) × r<sub>E</sub> + (D/V) × r<sub>D</sub> × (1 − t), with weights taken at <em>market</em> values.
    Worked example: market equity E = 800 at r<sub>E</sub> = 12%, debt D = 200 at r<sub>D</sub> = 6%, tax rate
    25%, so V = 1,000. Then WACC = 0.8 × 12% + 0.2 × 6% × 0.75 = 9.6% + 0.9% = <strong>10.5%</strong>. Notice
    the two classic slips: using book values instead of market values (a firm whose shares have tripled since
    issuance still gets weights from today's prices), and forgetting the (1 − t) factor on debt only — interest
    is the tax-deductible flow, dividends are not.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the hurdle rate is no longer an assumption from the NPV lesson — it
    is now sourced from the funding mix, with market-value weights and a tax shield on interest
    alone. Next comes the theorem that tells you what that mix is worth: in the perfect world,
    nothing.
  </p>

  <h2>Modigliani–Miller: the theorem that organises everything</h2>
  <p>
    In 1958 Modigliani and Miller proved something outrageous-sounding: in a perfect market — no taxes, no
    bankruptcy costs, no information problems — <strong>the value of a firm is independent of how it is
    financed</strong>. The intuition is the "homemade leverage" argument: if a levered firm were worth more
    than an unlevered one, an investor could borrow personally to buy the unlevered firm and replicate the
    levered payoffs more cheaply, so arbitrage closes the gap. The pie is the same size however you slice it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Their <strong>Proposition II</strong> explains why "debt is cheaper" is not a free lunch: the required
    return on equity rises linearly with leverage, r<sub>E</sub> = r<sub>A</sub> + (D/E) × (r<sub>A</sub> −
    r<sub>D</sub>), where r<sub>A</sub> is the firm's overall (unlevered) return. Take r<sub>A</sub> = 10% and
    r<sub>D</sub> = 6%. With no debt, equity holders get 10%. With D/E = 1 (half debt), they demand 10% +
    1 × 4% = <strong>14%</strong>. Check the blend: 0.5 × 14% + 0.5 × 6% = 10% — exactly unchanged. Debt is
    cheaper per unit, but it makes every remaining unit of equity riskier and more expensive, in precisely
    offsetting proportion.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="call the ratchet — what equity demands at D/E = 2" widget="estimate" config={cfPropIIEstimate} />
  <p>
    Everything real enters when you add back the frictions MM assumed away. With corporate taxes the levered
    firm is worth more: V<sub>L</sub> = V<sub>U</sub> + t<sub>C</sub> × D, because the government effectively
    co-invests in the debt portion — our example's 200 of debt shelters 200 × 25% = 50 of value. Against that
    stands the cost of <em>financial distress</em>: as leverage rises, the probability of bankruptcy and its
    direct (lawyers, fire sales) and indirect (customers flee, suppliers demand cash) costs eat the tax shield.
    Observed capital structures sit at the margin where the last unit of debt's shield equals its expected
    distress and agency cost.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "debt is cheaper than equity, so highly levered firms have a lower WACC and
  should borrow to the max." The first clause is true and the conclusion is false: leverage raises r<sub>E</sub>
  mechanically (Proposition II), and past some point expected distress costs raise the cost of debt too. Firms
  that lever up for the tax shield alone discover the shield is worthless precisely when cash flows — and hence
  taxable profits — disappear.</p>

  <h2>Leverage: magnifying both directions</h2>
  <p>
    Leverage is an amplifier with no polarity switch. Put 1,000 into a business earning EBIT of 120 (a 12%
    return on assets). Financed entirely with equity, the owners earn 12%. Financed with 500 of debt at 6%
    (interest 30) and 500 of equity, the good-year return on equity is (120 − 30) / 500 = <strong>18%</strong>.
    Now the downturn arrives and EBIT halves to 60: unlevered owners earn 6%, but levered owners earn
    (60 − 30) / 500 = <strong>6%</strong> — the amplifier gave them +6 points in the up year and took 6 away in
    the down one. The formal measure is the degree of financial leverage, DFL = EBIT / (EBIT − interest) =
    120 / 90 ≈ 1.33: every 10% fall in EBIT produces a 13.3% fall in earnings per share. The same logic runs at
    the operating line before any financing: high fixed costs create <em>operating</em> leverage, so a
    software firm (fixed costs high, marginal costs near zero) swings harder with demand than a corner shop.
    A firm with both leverages stacked is exposed to compounding shocks.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the investing rule is NPV at an honestly sourced hurdle, and
    leverage has been revealed as an amplifier with no polarity switch — it magnifies the good
    year and the bad identically. Next, the territory where profitable companies
    actually die: the gap between cash leaving and cash arriving.
  </p>

  <h2>Working capital: where firms actually die</h2>
  <p>
    Profitable companies go bust for one banal reason: cash arrives later than it must leave. The
    <strong>cash conversion cycle</strong> measures the gap. A firm holds inventory 60 days on average (DIO),
    collects from customers 45 days after sale (DSO), and pays suppliers after 40 days (DPO). Cash is locked up
    for 60 + 45 − 40 = <strong>65 days</strong>. On revenue of 7.3 million, that ties up roughly
    7.3 × 65 / 365 ≈ <strong>1.3 million</strong> of permanent funding. Squeeze DSO to 30 days and you release
    7.3 × 15 / 365 ≈ 0.3 million — free capital, no new investors required, which is why receivables discipline
    is the CFO's cheapest financing source.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="fill the cycle — where the cash sits" widget="cloze" config={cfCccCloze} />
  <p>
    Trade credit has a price too, and it is often astonishing. Terms of "2/10 net 30" mean: pay in 10 days for a
    2% discount, otherwise pay in full at day 30. Skipping the discount means paying 2 to borrow 98 for 20 extra
    days, an annualised cost of (2/98) × (365/20) ≈ <strong>37%</strong>. Any firm that can borrow at 10% should
    borrow and pay early. Managers who "stretch payables to help cash flow" are frequently borrowing at credit-card-and-worse rates without ever seeing the number on a statement.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a profitable company cannot run out of money." It seems
  reasonable — profit is the score everyone watches, and the accounts say the business earns more
  than it spends. But profit is an accrual concept: revenue is booked when earned, not when
  collected, and the cash conversion cycle shows exactly how long the gap runs — 65 days in this
  section's worked firm. A fast-growing profitable company pays wages, suppliers and taxes weeks
  before customers pay, and growth itself consumes cash. The correct picture: profitability and
  solvency are different axes, which is why squeezing DSO releases capital with no new investors
  and why stretched payables quietly cost credit-card rates. Firms die of the calendar, not the
  income statement.</p>

  <h2>Governance and payout: the people problem</h2>
  <p>
    Berle and Means pointed out in 1932 that the modern corporation separates ownership (dispersed shareholders)
    from control (professional managers), creating the <strong>agency problem</strong>: managers may prefer
    empires, perks, or quiet lives to shareholder value. Governance is the toolkit of responses — an independent
    board, audited accounts, incentive pay tied to value, debt that disciplines free cash flow, and the market
    for corporate control, where an under-managed firm's cheap shares invite a takeover. Every financing choice
    has a governance shadow: too much cash invites waste, too much debt invites under-investment in good projects,
    and payout policy is the pressure valve.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Dividends versus buybacks</strong> are, at fair prices, economically identical. A firm worth 100
    million with 1 million shares at 100 pays a 10 million dividend: the share price drops to 90 on the ex-date
    and the shareholder holds 90 plus 10 of cash. Instead it repurchases 100,000 shares at 100: 900,000 shares
    remain to own a 90 million firm — still worth 100 each. The differences are institutional, not arithmetic:
    dividends are a commitment (cutting one is read as distress), while buybacks are flexible and, in many tax
    systems, return cash as capital gains rather than income. Payout choices therefore signal: initiating a
    dividend says "this cash stream is durable," and large buybacks often say "our shares are cheap" — or,
    less happily, that managers have run out of positive-NPV projects. Modern buyback programs have grown to
    hundreds of billions of dollars a year among large US firms, which keeps the signalling debate live.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="drag the divider — dividend vs buyback" widget="compare" config={cfPayoutCompare} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Recall: state Modigliani–Miller Proposition I, and explain the homemade-leverage argument that proves it.</li>
    <li>Application: a firm earns EBIT of 120 with interest obligations of 30. Compute the degree of financial leverage and state what it implies if EBIT falls 10%.</li>
    <li>Why does the observed pattern — the most profitable firms carry the least debt — embarrass trade-off theory and support the pecking order?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) In a perfect market the value of a firm is independent of how
    it is financed. The proof: if a levered firm were worth more, an investor could borrow
    personally to buy the unlevered firm and replicate the levered payoffs more cheaply —
    arbitrage closes the gap, so the pie is the same size however sliced. (2) DFL = EBIT divided
    by EBIT minus interest = 120 over 90, about 1.33: every 10% fall in EBIT produces a 13.3%
    fall in earnings per share — the amplifier again, pointed down. (3) Trade-off
    theory says profitable firms should carry more debt — bigger tax shields, more distress
    capacity. The data show the opposite, which is what the pecking order predicts:
    information asymmetry makes equity a last resort, so firms with the most retained
    earnings need to issue least — internal cash first, then debt, equity last.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Project choice is NPV at an honestly sourced hurdle; PI ranks under rationing, and IRR misleads through its reinvestment assumption and sign changes.</li>
    <li>WACC blends debt and equity at market-value weights, applying the tax shield to interest only.</li>
    <li>Modigliani–Miller: in perfect markets capital structure is irrelevant — every real argument is a friction they assumed away.</li>
    <li>Leverage amplifies both directions; DFL = EBIT over EBIT minus interest quantifies the swing.</li>
    <li>Firms die of the calendar: the cash conversion cycle, not profit, decides survival.</li>
    <li>Trade credit has a price — skipping 2/10 net 30 costs about 37% annualised.</li>
    <li>Payout policy signals: dividends commit, buybacks flex.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The advanced lesson <em>Portfolio Theory (Markowitz)</em> sources the equity side of the
    WACC — risk-free rate, beta, the equity risk premium — while <em>Options and Derivatives</em>
    prices the real options this page's final section flagged: expand, delay or abandon rather
    than commit forever.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Two theories compete to explain real capital structures. The <strong>trade-off theory</strong> says firms
    balance tax shields against distress costs and drift toward a target leverage. The <strong>pecking order</strong>
    (Myers, 1984; Myers and Majluf, 1984) says information asymmetry dominates: managers know more than investors,
    so issuing equity when shares may be overvalued would punish existing holders; firms therefore fund projects
    first with retained earnings, then debt, and issue equity only as a last resort. The striking evidence — the
    most profitable firms, with the most internal cash, carry the <em>least</em> debt, the opposite of the
    trade-off prediction — favours the pecking order in many industries. Graham and Harvey's 2001 survey of CFOs
    found that financial flexibility and credit ratings, not tax shields, top their stated concerns; roughly a
    third target a leverage range, the rest do not.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three refinements take you to research level. <strong>Adjusted Present Value</strong> (Myers, 1974) values a
    project as if all-equity financed, then adds financing side-effects (tax shields, subsidised loans) separately —
    cleaner than forcing one WACC onto messy structures. <strong>The single-hurdle-rate error</strong>: firms that
    apply company-wide WACC to every project systematically over-invest in risky divisions and under-invest in safe
    ones, because a 10.5% hurdle is far too low for a speculative venture and far too high for replacing a
    dependable asset. <strong>Real options</strong>: standard NPV silently assumes you commit now and operate
    forever, ignoring the option to expand, delay, or abandon; where uncertainty is high and decisions can wait,
    the option value can overturn an NPV ranking.
  </p>
  <p>
    Open problems keep the field honest. Why do highly profitable firms hoard trillion-dollar cash piles — trade-off
    theory has no comfortable answer? How does monetary policy transmit through corporate balance sheets (the
    "financial accelerator" literature)? And does a lower cost of capital genuinely follow from good governance and
    ESG ratings, or does the observed spread reflect measurement error? Each is a live research program you can
    now read the literature on.
  </p>

